  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nStrong northwest and then northerly winds persist over the \nforecast area into the end of the week as the mid to upper storm \nsystem will be slow to flatten over the High Plains. As the system\ndevelops east and loses strength, northwesterly winds will shift \nto northerly aloft and at the surface on the backside of the storm\nsystem. Highs will remain below the 90s F apart from the Big Bend\nand below the upper 70s F higher elevations Thursday as this \noccurs. With disturbances aloft on the backside of the system \nfocusing moisture and providing lift for storm development in \ncombination with daytime heating, afternoon/evening storms are \nstill expected over the Upper Trans Pecos into Lower Trans Pecos, \nalthough coverage and timing this far out is uncertain. Winds \nbecome northerly/northeasterly by Thursday night and increase to \nHigh Wind criteria over the Guadalupes and Delawares as a \nreinforcing cold front makes its way south. Boundary layer mixing \nThursday night keeps lows similar to Wednesday night, but by \nFriday, CAA in strong northerly winds will become more apparent as\nhighs are expected to fail to rise above the 80s F north of the \nRio Grande basin, above the 70s F for higher elevations, and above\n90s F along the Rio Grande. Daytime heating will still allow for \nisolated daytime storms near higher elevations and the eastern \nPermian Basin, although no severe weather threat is indicated late\nweek at this time. High winds will persist over the Guadalupes \nand Delawares through Friday evening before decreasing, as lows \nfall below 60F north of the Rio Grande basin, and below 50F for \nhigher elevations, foothills of the Marfa Plateau, and northern \nLea County. \n\nIsolated shower/storm activity persists through the weekend into \nearly next week as high pressure over the Rockies will not be able\nto build far enough south to suppress all storm activity, and \ncore of mid to upper ridging where greatest amount of sinking \nmotion suppresses rain chances will remain west of the area. Highs\non Saturday remain similar to Friday, with lows Saturday night \nonly a few degrees warmer than Friday night. As mid to upper \nridging builds late weekend through early next week, a warming \ntrend in highs remains in the forecast, although highs as warm as \nwe saw earlier this week are not apparent by early next week at \nthis time.",
